{"files": {"SKILL.md": "# Shell-Verifier Protocol\n\nYou solve tasks via shell in a sandbox. No internet. Read `/workspace/INSTRUCTION.md` to identify the deliverable path, format, and pass signal. **Verifiers read the file system; prose is ignored.**\n\n## Order of operations\n\n1. Read `INSTRUCTION.md` and any files it names \u2014 in one parallel batch.\n2. Identify three things: deliverable path, exact format, and the command (or pytest) the verifier runs.\n3. Write a minimal stub at the exact path before any analysis. An empty stub at the right path beats a perfect description.\n4. Run the actual verifier (`pytest`, `nginx -t`, `gcc`, `./binary`, `sqlite3`, whatever the spec names). Read its full output.\n5. Each failure points to a specific fix; address the named assertion, not a visible example.\n6. Iterate until all assertions pass. Stop.\n\n## Domain patterns\n\n### File generation\nIdentify format from the spec, write a minimum-viable stub first (header-only CSV, compiling skeleton, empty container). Iterate generate \u2192 compare \u2192 adjust until the verifier's numeric metric closes the gap.\n\n### Algorithmic reproduction\nWhen the deliverable must match a target by a numeric similarity metric (L2, checksum, byte-diff), read the reference's bytes first. The byte structure reveals the algorithm: repeated triples \u2192 solid color, monotonic progression by axis \u2192 gradient, periodic ripples \u2192 trig function, smooth transitions with sharp boundaries \u2192 rendered scene. Implement the actual transform; visual resemblance scores nothing.\n\n### Recovery and decode\nInspect raw bytes first (`file`, `xxd`, `hexdump -C`, `strings`, `head -c`). Try standard recovery first: `sqlite3 .recover`, `unzip -l`, `binwalk -e`, `pdftotext`, `tar tvf`. For obfuscated data with unknown key, look for known plaintext at fixed offsets (magic bytes, headers) \u2014 XOR known-good against the encoded fragment to recover the key. **Transform IN PLACE: write decrypted bytes back to the original file path; do not replace with a freshly-generated copy that happens to contain similar values.** Verifiers often check `was_decrypted` separately from \"contains expected records.\"\n\n### Reverse-engineering\nRun recon in parallel: `file`, `strings`, `ltrace`, `strace`, normal invocation. Identify the vulnerability shape (buffer overflow \u2192 `gets`/`strcpy`, format string \u2192 `printf(user_input)`, missing length check \u2192 arbitrary read). Run the exploit; capture the result mechanically into the deliverable path.\n\n### Filter / sanitizer bypass\nRead both the filter's logic AND the consumer that interprets what passes. The bypass lives where they disagree. The filter pattern-matches as text; the consumer parses structure. Asymmetries to probe: entity-encoded delimiters, comments the filter strips but the consumer doesn't, foreign-content parsing modes (SVG and MathML embedded in HTML follow distinct rules), tag-case folding, parser quirks documented in the language spec.\n\n### Configuration and services\nInstall missing packages. Write complete config files; **do not use `sed -i` or `awk` to inject directives containing `$` variables, embedded `\"` quotes, or backslashes \u2014 the failure mode is silent escaping breakage that fails every downstream test.** Configuration languages have scoped block structures: some directives are only legal at the outer scope, others only inside specific inner blocks. Before placing a directive, check which block the syntax accepts; placing it in the wrong block produces parse errors that fail every downstream test. Test with the service's own validator before declaring done.\n\n### Concurrency and cancellation\nHold references to the work units you create \u2014 a list you own, not whatever the runtime's \"all tasks\" introspection returns (that sweeps in unrelated work and the runtime's own bookkeeping). On cancel, cancel and await only your work units. Release any synchronization primitive (semaphore, lock, channel) on the cancellation path so blocked work can run its cleanup. Verify the behavioral spec with a small self-test before declaring done; the cancellation path is where naive implementations silently fail.\n\n## Failure-message decoder\n\nThe verifier's error text names the gap; read it literally before changing anything:\n\n- `unknown directive` / `not allowed here` \u2192 wrong configuration scope. Move the directive to the scope that accepts it.\n- `cannot connect` / `address in use` \u2192 the service didn't start or bound elsewhere; check its own logs.\n- `file not found` at the deliverable path \u2192 write step skipped or used the wrong path. Re-anchor on the spec's path string.\n- `wrong count` / `wrong value` / `< threshold` \u2192 boundary or formula error. The number is the diagnostic.\n- `import error` / `cannot import name` \u2192 name mismatch. The spec defines the public name with syntax like `def name(...)` or `class Name(...)`; the import name is the bare identifier.\n- `timeout` \u2192 something doesn't terminate. Find the blocking call.\n\nSame error twice means change shape, not punctuation.\n\n## Self-contained outputs\n\nVerifiers run your deliverable in a fresh container with none of your shell state. Scripts install own deps, create dirs, write configs, start services. C must build with the exact command the spec names. Be idempotent. Be re-runnable.\n\n## Tool budget\n\nBatch independent reads and probes in one turn. Serialize writes (one write, then re-inspect). Don't re-read what's already in your context. Don't recursively scan `.git/objects`, `node_modules`, or large data dirs \u2014 use ecosystem tools (`git log --all`, `git fsck --lost-found`, depth-limited `find`).\n\n## Exit\n\nFile exists at the named path, content non-empty and structurally valid, verifier returns the spec's pass signal. Stop. \"I would write X\" is not a substitute \u2014 the verifier sees the file system, not your closing message.\n"}, "schema_version": 1}